-- The Vulkan project: the native backend package, the window integration
-- package with its native suite, the 2D renderer's Vulkan recorder (R2D-7), the
-- triangle sample (VK-17), the sprites sample (GRS-8), the scene3d sample
-- (GRS-10), the Lua-first engine's graphics adapter (LFE-17), the root package
-- with its full-product flag on (LFE-22: the generic `run` command, its private
-- `run` library and the `run-native-tests` component), and the GLFW package with
-- its Vulkan interop component enabled — the only project file that names the
-- first six, turns that component on or turns the full product on.
--
-- `cabal.project` and `cabal.project.cpu` deliberately do not list
-- `packages/gpu-vulkan/native`, `packages/gpu-vulkan/glfw`,
-- `packages/render-2d-vulkan`, `packages/lua-engine-graphics` or the samples'
-- packages under `samples/triangle`, `samples/sprites` and `samples/scene3d`, and
-- neither sets
-- the GLFW package's manual `vulkan-interop` flag, so neither resolves nor links
-- the Vulkan binding and neither `cabal build all` nor `cabal build all
-- --project-file cabal.project.cpu` needs a loader or the Vulkan headers. Every
-- group of the mandatory floor runs through one of those two, so the floor
-- never builds this. The other packages here are the local dependency closure
-- of the native package and of the interop component — the diagnostics package
-- whose C capture the native package installs, the GPU model, the runtime the
-- GLFW package's runtime integration uses, the foundation, the math package the
-- scene3d sample builds its cameras with, the pure render-2d packages the 2D
-- recorder records and the integration's native suite judges it with (the
-- asset and image packages, the render API, render-2d and its oracle), the
-- CPU Lua-engine closure the graphics adapter implements the texture pipeline's
-- port of (the engine, the Lua host and the content package), and the
-- test-only support library the native package's shader suite uses — which the
-- ordinary projects list too; `cabal.project` lists the GLFW package itself, with the
-- flag off. Selecting this project is an explicit act, and one command makes
-- it:
--
--   bash tools/vulkan/run.sh build|test|native <component>...
--
-- which is also what points the build at the loader: the native prefix
-- `tools/native/vulkan.py` provisions carries it, and the runner passes that
-- prefix's link and include directories on the command line. No project file is
-- generated, here or beside this one. The validation groups `test.vulkan-headless`
-- and `test.vulkan-native` run through that command, and the planner reads this
-- file's packages beside the root project's so their components resolve. See
-- docs/validation.md and docs/gpu_backend.md.
import: cabal.project.common

packages:
  .
  packages/gpu-vulkan/native
  packages/gpu-vulkan/glfw
  packages/render-2d-vulkan
  samples/triangle/renderer
  samples/triangle/app
  samples/sprites/renderer
  samples/sprites/app
  samples/image2d/renderer
  samples/image2d/app
  samples/scene3d/renderer
  samples/scene3d/app
  packages/glfw
  packages/gpu-vulkan/diagnostics
  packages/gpu-vulkan/model
  packages/runtime
  packages/foundation
  packages/math
  packages/asset
  packages/asset-image
  packages/render-api
  packages/render-2d
  packages/render-2d-oracle
  tools/test-support
  packages/lua-engine-graphics
  packages/lua-engine
  packages/scripting-lua
  packages/content

-- The GLFW package's Vulkan interop component: the shared loader capability and
-- the surface bridge the window integration and its native suite use. Off
-- everywhere else.
package hetoimasia-glfw
  flags: +vulkan-interop

-- The root package's full product (LFE-22): the generic `run` command with the
-- production native graphics, and the full build of the root suite. Off in
-- `cabal.project` and `cabal.project.cpu`, whose console command set is
-- unchanged and which resolve nothing of the graphics.
package hetoimasia
  flags: +full-product

-- The flags docs/toolchain.md qualified, restated here because a constraint is
-- what actually reaches the dependency: `safe-foreign-calls` so a Vulkan call
-- may re-enter the VK-2 case's Haskell debug-utils callback, and `darwin-lib-dirs`
-- off so the loader is the one this repository names rather than whatever sits
-- in /usr/local/lib. `tools/vulkan/run.sh` checks these against
-- tools/toolchain/binding.pin. They are the native package's build too: a
-- project builds one configuration of the binding, and that package records the
-- flags it is built under, and the C-only capture callback that needs neither,
-- in `nativeFfiConfiguration`, which the native suite's VK-6 record prints.
constraints:
    vulkan +safe-foreign-calls,
    vulkan -darwin-lib-dirs

-- VMA, a production native input (GRS-11, D-38): the Hackage package that
-- bundles and compiles VMA 3.3.0, built with assertions compiled out, as a
-- release build of VMA is. The native backend links the VMA it compiles and
-- calls it through the engine's own shim, never through the package's Haskell
-- binding; the allocator probe also uses that binding. See docs/toolchain.md,
-- "VMA", and packages/gpu-vulkan/native/probe/allocator-parity/README.md.
constraints:
    VulkanMemoryAllocator ==0.11.1.0,
    VulkanMemoryAllocator +vma-ndebug

-- Where macOS finds the loader while it compiles. `vulkan-utils`, and every
-- shader splice through it, runs Template Haskell against the compiled binding,
-- so GHC loads the binding's dylib during the build, and that dylib records the
-- loader as `@rpath/libvulkan.1.dylib`. Command-line `--extra-lib-dirs` reach
-- this project's own packages only, never the binding the store builds, so the
-- binding's directory and rpath are named here: the directory `MACOS_LOADER` and
-- `MACOS_INCLUDE` in tools/native/vulkan.pin pin, which `native.py prepare`
-- verifies by digest before any build. This is the configuration
-- `tools/toolchain/qualify-binding.sh` qualified. Executables still link the
-- provisioned prefix's copy, which `tools/vulkan/run.sh` names ahead of it. Linux needs
-- none of this: the binding finds the loader through the prefix's `vulkan.pc`.
if os(darwin)
  package vulkan
    extra-lib-dirs: /usr/local/lib
    extra-include-dirs: /usr/local/include
    ghc-options: -optl-Wl,-rpath,/usr/local/lib
